---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import FileDiagram from '../components/FileDiagram.astro';
import WindowsDiagram from '../components/WindowsDiagram.astro';
import AttackerDiagram from '../components/AttackerDiagram.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import ResearchCallout from '../components/ResearchCallout.astro';
import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
---
<Layout title="File protection architecture">
  <PageHero eyebrow="THE TECHNOLOGY" title="Look inside the protection." text="Storage access is not file access. Readable data requires the correct protected fragments, protected cryptographic material and the authorized Cyber Reliant reconstruction path." number="04"/>
  <section class="section container split-content" id="data-centric"><div><span class="eyebrow">DATA-CENTRIC PROTECTION</span><h2>Protect the information.<br/>Not just its surroundings.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">Infrastructure access does not automatically become file access.</p><p>Cyber Reliant fragments sensitive files, encrypts the fragments and separates protected data from cryptographic material. Reconstruction requires both through the authorized access path.</p><p>Protection at this layer complements the identity, endpoint, network and recovery controls around it. It is designed to integrate with existing environments.</p><a class="text-link" href="/how-it-works">Trace the four-part mechanism <Arrow/></a></div></section>
  <section class="soft-section" id="zero-trust"><div class="section container split-content"><div><span class="eyebrow">ZERO TRUST, EXTENDED TO THE DATA ITSELF</span><h2>Make the access<br/>decision explicit.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">Reconstruction belongs to an authorized access path—not every process that can reach storage.</p><p>Network access is not automatic data access, and storage access is not automatic access to readable files. Application policy participates in the authorization decision. The authorized Cyber Reliant path identifies, decrypts and reconstructs the protected file.</p><a class="text-link" href="/request-demo?interest=Technical%20architecture">Discuss your trust boundaries <Arrow/></a><details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Where this fits in a zero-trust architecture</summary><p>File protection is one part of a broader architecture. It does not replace identity, device, network or operational controls, or establish a complete zero-trust architecture by itself.</p></details></div></div></section>

  <section class="section container" id="quantum"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">QUANTUM / AIA / INFORMATION-THEORETIC SECURITY</span><h2>Protect against<br/>harvest now, decrypt later.</h2></div><p>Address both the strength of the encryption and the protected components an attacker would need to obtain.</p></div>
    <div class="quantum-comparison"><article><span class="eyebrow">COMPUTATIONAL DIFFICULTY</span><h3>How hard is it to decrypt?</h3><p>Traditional encryption generally relies on the mathematical problem protecting the key remaining computationally difficult. Quantum computing changes assumptions for some cryptographic systems, creating risk for data collected today and retained for future decryption.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">AVAILABILITY OF REQUIRED INFORMATION</span><h3>What must an attacker possess?</h3><p>FDD’s research calls the evaluated strategy Augmented Improbability of Access (AIA). It applies principles of information-theoretic security: an attacker must obtain sufficient protected data fragments and cryptographic components before reconstruction or decryption is possible.</p></article></div>
    <div class="split-content quantum-explanation"><div><h3>Separate the information required to reconstruct.</h3><p>The research describes fragmenting both data and the key that decrypts it, distributing protected components across platforms so no single location contains the complete required set. This provides a conceptual basis for the separate file and key paths illustrated below.</p></div><div><h3>Protection grounded in information-theoretic principles.</h3><p>Information-theoretic security is intended to remain effective even against unlimited time and computing power, within the assumptions of its model. The approach focuses on the information required for reconstruction, alongside the computational strength of encryption.</p><p>Component separation, authorization, endpoint security and recovery work together within the deployment’s threat model.</p></div></div>
    <ResearchCallout/>
  </section>
  <section class="section container" id="windows">
    <div class="section-heading windows-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">WINDOWS IMPLEMENTATION</span><h2>The filter doesn’t just intercept the file.<br/>It participates in rebuilding it.</h2></div><p>Cyber Reliant’s file-system filter participates in the information and processes required to turn protected fragments back into a readable file.</p></div>
    <WindowsDiagram/>
    <div class="attacker-section" id="attacker-path"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">A DIFFERENT DRIVER IS NOT THE SAME PROCESS</span><h2>Reaching storage<br/>doesn’t rebuild the file.</h2></div><p>Replacing or bypassing a conventional file-system driver does not, by itself, reproduce Cyber Reliant reconstruction.</p></div><AttackerDiagram/>
      <details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Threat-model boundary: driver replacement and kernel-level attacks</summary><p>Storage access is distinct from the information, cryptographic material and protected processes needed for reconstruction. Application identity, key access, readable data in memory and kernel-level threats remain part of the security model.</p></details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="dark-section" id="network"><div class="container section network-detail">
    <div class="network-intro"><div><span class="outline-tag">UPCOMING NETWORK VERSION</span><h2>The data path and the key path<br/>are separate by design.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">The architecture fragments the cryptographic material itself.</p><p>Encrypted file fragments are distributed across logical storage locations. Separately, cryptographic material is fragmented or shredded, and multiple key components are protected through the HSM architecture. Both paths come together through authorized Cyber Reliant reconstruction.</p></div></div>
    <div class="network-visual"><FileDiagram compact/></div>
    <details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Network architecture details</summary><p>The upcoming network architecture separates protected file fragments and cryptographic components. Component counts are illustrative. Logical storage separation does not necessarily imply physically independent failure domains. HSM integration, key lifecycle and recovery are deployment-specific design considerations.</p></details>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">SECURITY IS IN THE DETAILS</span><h2>Understand the complete<br/>protection path.</h2></div></div><div class="technical-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Technical evaluation table, scroll horizontally for all columns"><table class="technical-table"><caption>Architecture and deployment considerations</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Architecture</th><th scope="col">Deployment considerations</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">File path</th><td>Fragment the file, encrypt its fragments and distribute the protected fragments</td><td>Algorithms, correct fragment identification, integrity controls, dependencies and logical storage boundaries</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Network key path <span class="table-status">Upcoming</span></th><td>Fragment/shred cryptographic material and protect multiple key components through the HSM architecture</td><td>Component protection, permitted operations, rotation, revocation, recovery and the HSM integration</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Authorized reconstruction</th><td>Bring required file fragments and protected cryptographic material together through the Cyber Reliant access path</td><td>Authorization, decryption, reconstruction behavior and readable-data exposure during use</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Windows filter</th><td>Participate in identifying fragments, obtaining cryptographic material, decrypting and rebuilding the file</td><td>Supported Windows versions, replacement-filter scenarios, application identity, kernel-level threat model and performance</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Operational resilience</th><td>Separate stored file fragments from the complete reconstruction path</td><td>Backup/restore behavior, fragment or key-component loss, storage outages and incident procedures</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div></section>

  <CTA title="Go deeper than the diagram." text="Walk through the file path, the key path and the reconstruction process against your threat model."/>
</Layout>
